// Reserved API shapes for the SLA goroutine syntax batch (the
// runtime mechanisms exist in this phase; the registration
// externs land with the syntax batch so the runtime needs no
// rework):
//
// 1. Boundary trap catch point (panic/recover supervision
//    semantics): every goroutine runs under a trampoline that
//    arms a rescue frame before invoking the entry point.
//    Runtime-raised traps (send on closed channel, negative
//    WaitGroup counter, double close) record {code, message} in
//    the goroutine and resume the rescue frame instead of
//    unwinding; the boundary then invokes the goroutine's trap
//    sink (or the default process trap when none is registered),
//    and sa_task_join reports SA_TASK_TRAPPED (100). The syntax
//    batch adds: a spawn variant taking a trap-sink function
//    pointer + context, and sa_task_trap_info(handle, &code,
//    &msg_ptr, &msg_len) to read the record back after join.
//    Stack overflow is NOT boundary-recoverable (the stack is
//    gone); it stays a loud process trap.
//
// 2. Monitor (Erlang-style exit notification): goroutine exit —
//    normal or trapped — converges on a single lifecycle point
//    in the runtime, after the trap record is final and before
//    join waiters fire. That is the production point for exit
//    notifications: a subscription registered on a goroutine
//    gets a {handle, exit kind, trap code} message enqueued to
//    the subscriber's channel there, receivable through ordinary
//    channel/select waits. The syntax batch adds the
//    subscription extern (spawn-monitored / monitor-register)
//    mirroring the trap-sink shape.
//
// 3. Context-style cancellation is a std idiom, not a mechanism:
//    a capacity-0 channel closed to broadcast "done" — closing
//    wakes every blocked receiver and every select case on it,
//    so a Done channel can be selected on alongside work
//    channels and passed down a call chain.
